Subject: Re: Post-demo: SDK auth + intermittent 5xxs (recap & next steps)

Alex — digging into the traces you sent, two possible candidates:

1) Routing fallbacks: if the primary reranker shard is under pressure it may route to a slower replica and the gateway can return 502/504 under heavy concurrency. You can force the X-Redwood-Model header to pick a known-good shard while we investigate.

2) Payload size + structured output: when structured output/tool-calling is enabled, the post-processing step can increase compute and memory footprint. Large schemas or long tool outputs may blow past internal soft limits and cause an upstream process to abort (5xx).

Repro snippet (Node):

const { RedwoodClient } = require('@redwood/sdk');
const client = new RedwoodClient({ token: process.env.RW_TOKEN, timeout: 15000 });

(async () => {
  try {
    const resp = await client.rerank({
      model: 'redwood-reranker-v1-stable',
      items: require('./sample-payload.json').items,
      top_k: 5,
      enable_structured_output: true,
      headers: { 'X-Redwood-Route': 'fixed:rerank-1' }
    });
    console.log(resp);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('ERR', err.response?.status, err.message);
  }
})();

Notes:
- Set REDWOOD_LOG_LEVEL=debug on your client to include request ids.
- If you can repro with the model pinned and a 30s client timeout, send us the request ids for the failed calls and we'll pull the server trace.

I'll open INF-3127 internally to track. If the customer is comfortable, we can request ephemeral debug keys to trace server-side behavior.

